{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"05","section":"06","chapter_title":"AI-Enhanced Presentation Development","title":"Citation and Attribution","description":"CAT 100 textbook section on citation and attribution, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the citation and attribution checklist shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development","cat100_supplement_citation_and_attribution","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/05/05"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_calibration"]},"content":"# Citation and Attribution\n\n![Citation and Attribution textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/05-06-citation-and-attribution.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA presentation makes claims, borrows charts, and increasingly leans on AI to draft text. Each of those is a debt to a source, and citation is how you record the debt so a skeptical viewer can check it. \"Found online\" is not a citation; it is an admission that you did not trace the source. A figure with no verifiable origin is a figure the audience has no reason to trust, and an AI-drafted paragraph passed off as your own is a different problem entirely, an integrity failure rather than a sloppiness one.\n\nThis section teaches the practical mechanics of attribution on a slide: tracing a borrowed asset to its real source, checking its license, verifying that the number you quote actually appears where you point, and disclosing AI assistance where it is required. The artifact is a privacy-and-attribution checklist that makes each of those checks visible.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the citation and attribution checklist shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student drops a striking unemployment chart onto a slide; her note to herself says \"found on Google.\" During the presentation a faculty member asks, \"What year is that data, and who published it?\" She cannot answer, and the strong claim built on the chart collapses in front of the room. A classmate who captioned the same chart \"U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, 2025 (public domain, U.S. government work)\" answers the question in one breath, and his claim stands. The difference was not design; it was whether the source could be traced.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAttribution answers three separate questions, and weak slides confuse them:\n\n- **Where did this come from?** The source: who produced the asset and when. A chart, a photo, a statistic, and a quotation each need a traceable origin, not \"online.\"\n- **Am I allowed to use it this way?** The license. A CC BY image can be reused with credit; an \"all rights reserved\" image cannot be reposted just because you found it. Public-domain government data (like BLS releases) is broadly reusable but still must be cited so the audience can verify it.\n- **Is the number actually there?** Verification. Citing a page is not enough if the figure you quote does not appear on it. Point to the exact table or line that states the value.\n\nAI assistance adds a fourth obligation: **disclosure**. If AI drafted or substantially shaped your text, and the assignment or context expects you to say so, you disclose it. Disclosure is not a confession of weakness; it is the honest labeling of who wrote what, the same way a citation labels who produced a chart. The line that matters: citation makes a borrowed *fact or asset* checkable, while disclosure makes the *authorship* of your own slide honest. Both are required, and they are not the same move. Disclosure wording should follow your course's stated AI-use policy or syllabus statement; expectations are evolving and vary by instructor. Current style guides give concrete models: APA (updated September 2025) cites the specific AI chat with the company as author, the model named, and a date, and MLA recommends naming the prompt, tool, model, and date.\n\nThe course anchor doing the work here is [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students), which treats verifiable sourcing and honest disclosure as conditions for participating in a shared information space, not as bureaucratic box-checking.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nGoal: attribute a reused chart and an AI-drafted summary correctly on one slide.\n\nStarting state (under-credited):\n\n```\n[chart image]          <- \"found online\"\nSummary paragraph      <- drafted by AI, lightly reworded\n```\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Trace the chart to its real source and check the license. It is from the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, 2025, in the public domain as a U.S. government work (cite BLS as the source).\n2. Add a caption that names source, year, and license:\n\n   `Chart: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, 2025 (public domain, U.S. government work).`\n\n3. Verify the figure you quote actually appears at the linked page. The 18% you cited was not on that page, so swap the link to the exact table that states it.\n4. Disclose AI assistance where the assignment requires it:\n\n   `Summary drafted with AI assistance (e.g., ChatGPT, Claude, or Copilot; [date]), edited and verified by me.`\n\nStep 3 is the one students skip and reviewers catch. The chart had a real source, but the specific 18 percent figure lived on a different table than the page you linked, which means a checker following your citation would not find your number. Citing the right document but the wrong page is a near-miss that still fails verification.\n\nWork one attribution decision at a time, predicting each move before you reveal it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Attribute a borrowed chart and an AI-drafted summary\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Your note under the chart says \\\"found online.\\\" What is the first thing to establish?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A nicer caption font\",\"The real source and the license, who produced it, when, and whether you may reuse it\",\"Whether the chart matches your slide theme\",\"A larger version of the image\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Trace the source and license: U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, 2025, in the public domain as a U.S. government work (cite BLS as the source). \\\"Found online\\\" is not a citation; it is an admission you did not trace it.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You add the caption and link the BLS homepage. Is the figure now verifiable?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Yes; the homepage proves the source\",\"No; the link must reach the exact table where your 18 percent figure actually appears\",\"Yes, as long as the license is correct\",\"No; you must also screenshot the page\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Not yet. A checker following the link must land on the precise table that states 18 percent, not a homepage. Re-point the link to the exact figure.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Disclose AI authorship where expected: \\\"Summary drafted with AI assistance (e.g., ChatGPT, Claude, or Copilot; [date]), edited and verified by me.\\\" Citation makes the borrowed asset checkable; disclosure makes your own authorship honest. Both are required and they are different moves.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A student lightly rewords an AI-drafted paragraph and argues that paraphrasing removes the need to disclose. Why is that wrong?\" explanation=\"Paraphrasing changes the words, not the authorship. If AI shaped the content and disclosure is expected, rewording does not cancel the obligation to label who wrote what.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Rewording makes the text original, so no disclosure is needed\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Paraphrasing changes the words, not the authorship; disclosure is still required\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Disclosure is only for borrowed charts, not text\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"AI text never needs disclosure\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nTrace every borrowed asset to its real source, confirm the license permits your use, verify the exact figure where you point, and disclose AI authorship where it is expected. If a viewer cannot follow your citation to the precise claim, the citation is incomplete.\n\nCheck a slide's attribution against three questions:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** Could a skeptical viewer reach the original source and confirm it is yours to use this way?\n2. **Evidence check:** Does the exact number, quote, or asset appear at the precise location you cite, not just on the general site?\n3. **Revision check:** What did you re-source, relicense-check, or disclose, and what suggestion did you reject?\n\nIf the citation lands on a homepage rather than the specific table or figure, tighten it before you present.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is that linking the general website counts as citing the figure. A viewer who follows the link must land where the number actually is; a homepage link makes the claim unverifiable in practice.\n\nThe second is that lightly rewording AI-drafted text removes the need to disclose. Paraphrasing changes the words, not the authorship; if AI shaped the content and disclosure is expected, rewording does not cancel the obligation.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the four questions to a quotation rather than a chart. You want to quote a researcher's line on your slide. Source: name the author and the work, not \"an expert said.\" License and use: a short attributed quotation for commentary is generally fine, but reproducing a long passage may not be, so you keep it brief. Verification: paste the exact words and confirm they match the original, because misquoting an attributed source is worse than not quoting at all. Disclosure: if AI helped you find or summarize the quote, you still verified the original yourself, and you say so if required.\n\nNow suppose the same slide also uses an AI-generated image. The attribution logic forks: a borrowed human-made asset needs source, license, and verification, while an AI-generated image needs a different note (\"image generated with AI\"), because there is no external creator to credit but the audience still deserves to know it is synthetic. One slide, two kinds of honesty: crediting what you borrowed and labeling what a machine produced.\n\nOrder the attribution pass so each check depends on the one before it:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Trace each borrowed asset to its real source: who produced it and when\", \"Check the license to confirm your specific reuse is allowed\", \"Verify the exact figure or quote appears at the precise place you cite\", \"Re-point any link that lands on a homepage instead of the figure\", \"Disclose where AI drafted or substantially shaped your own text\", \"Record the re-source, the wrong-page fix, and any rejected suggestion\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: on the left, each borrowed or AI-touched element on the slide; on the right, its source, license, verification location, and any disclosure. Flag anything marked \"found online\" or \"AI-drafted\" without a check. By the end of 05.06, your note should let a reviewer follow every claim to its origin.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. For each asset, who produced it and when?\n2. Does the license allow your specific use?\n3. Does the exact figure appear at the precise place you cite?\n4. Where did AI shape the content, and is that disclosed?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe checklist is strong when a reviewer can trace every borrowed asset to its source and license, confirm each quoted figure at its exact location, and see where AI assistance is disclosed. Treat a polished caption as worthless if the link behind it does not reach the claim.\n\nA useful entry would sound like this: \"I replaced 'found online' with 'U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics, 2025 (public domain, U.S. government work)' and re-pointed the link from the homepage to the exact table, because my 18 percent figure was not on the page I first cited. I disclosed that the summary was AI-drafted and verified by me, and I rejected a suggestion to drop the disclosure to look more original, because that would misrepresent authorship.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same content into a printed handout with no clickable links. Attribution must still be traceable, so the caption now needs enough text for a reader to find the source without a hyperlink: full publisher, year, table number, and access note. The verification burden does not shrink because the medium changed; it grows, because the reader cannot click through. If your citation works on paper, it was a real citation, not a link standing in for one.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nConnect the ideas this section builds on. See how source, license, verification, and disclosure feed evidence-based revision of the attribution checklist:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_citation_and_attribution\", \"cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the citation and attribution checklist packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/05-06-citation-and-attribution-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"citation and attribution checklist\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"05.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation of source, license, verification, and disclosure as four separate checks.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a \"found online\" caption with a source-year-license caption that reaches the exact table.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the four checks to a quotation or an AI-generated image.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where verifying a link exposed a wrong-page citation.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the citation and attribution checklist for CAT 100 05.06. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_citation_and_attribution\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm every borrowed asset names a source and license, every quoted figure is verifiable at its exact location, and AI assistance is disclosed where it is expected.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n- [APA Style, citing generative AI (updated September 2025)](https://apastyle.apa.org/blog/cite-generative-ai-references)\n- [MLA Style Center, How do I cite generative AI in MLA style?](https://style.mla.org/citing-generative-ai-updated-revised/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_citation_and_attribution","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_citation_and_attribution","cat100_supplement_ai_enhanced_presentation_development"],"stem":"Your slide uses a chart you found online and a paragraph drafted by AI. Which attribution shows you handled credit correctly?","options":["No source line, because the chart was free to view.","A caption: 'Chart: U.S. BLS, 2025 (public domain)' and a note 'Summary drafted with AI, edited by me' where disclosure is required.","A line saying 'image from the internet.'","Nothing, since you reworded the AI text slightly."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Correct attribution names the real source and license for the media and discloses AI assistance where required.","difficulty":"easy","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_image_no_credit"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"Which note gives BigAL a concrete attribution problem to check?","options":["My citations are fine.","Slide 4 cites a 2025 report URL, but the page does not contain the 18% figure I quoted. Did I cite the wrong source?","Add some citations.","I think I cited stuff."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The note names a specific mismatch between the claim and the linked source, so feedback can verify it.","difficulty":"medium","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_citation_is_decoration"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"stem":"What is a defensible way to use AI when handling citations?","options":["Ask AI to generate citations and trust them without checking the sources exist.","Ask AI to format a reference you supply, then verify the link actually contains the claim and disclose AI help where required.","Let AI invent plausible-looking sources to fill the reference list.","Use AI to remove all citations so the slides look cleaner."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI can format references you provide, but you must verify the source and disclose AI use; fabricated citations are an integrity violation.","difficulty":"hard","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_ai_not_disclosed"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_5_6_citation_and_attribution_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"evidence_based_revision","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"A reviewer says one citation is 'just a URL.' Which revision makes it verifiable?","options":["Bold the URL.","Replace it with 'Author/Org, title, year, and a link to the exact page that states the 18% figure.'","Move the URL to the last slide.","Change the URL font color."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A verifiable citation names author, title, year, and points to the exact location of the claim, not a bare link.","difficulty":"medium"}]},"content_version":{"content_version":"0623e5dd56a8ed13cbc5bb4215e50cb0c9f0ace9d68552453424728006b5d809","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}